\f1\fs22 The first person to starve to death while holding a fully charged uGrasp tablet caused remarkably little concern at Grasp Incorporated because he had been on the free plan.\
There were meetings, naturally.\
There were graphs.\
A task force spent three weeks determining that the deceased had received precisely the nutritional support promised under the terms and conditions of u		Grasp Sustain Basic, which was not, the task force pointed out, the same thing as receiving enough food.\
The distinction was important.\
Enough food had been part of the original product.\
In the early days, when Grasp had still been trying to persuade people that eating through their fingers was preferable to eating through their mouths, every device supplied one hundred per cent of an adult human\'92s daily nutritional requirements. Glucose, protein, fats, amino acids, vitamins, electrolytes and most of the things people had previously obtained through an enormously complicated process involving farms, trucks, kitchens, plates and chewing.\
The screen delivered everything transdermally.\
You touched it.\
It fed you.\
The public had been sceptical for nearly six weeks.\
Then Grasp added dopamine.\
Restaurants disappeared more slowly than expected. Most survived by becoming entertainment venues where people paid to watch professional chefs set fire to extinct ingredients. Cutlery acquired the status of antique weaponry. Wealthy households displayed dining tables in the same spirit that an earlier generation might have displayed suits of armour: evidence that their ancestors had once done something dangerous indoors.\
Grasp prospered.\
The nutritional service did not remain complete.\
It became 
\f2\i customisable
\f1\i0 .\
The first thing Grasp removed was dietary fibre, on the reasonable grounds that customers kept complaining about what it did. Fibre returned three months later as Digestive+, a premium feature advertised by a smiling woman sitting confidently on a white sofa.\
Iron was moved into Women\'92s Wellness, then restored to men after a number of them became too tired to buy things.\
Vitamin D became SunInside.\
Omega-3 became BrainShine.\
Salt was briefly sponsored.\
Protein remained free longer than most nutrients because nobody in Marketing had found a way to explain removing it without customers noticing their arms.\
Water survived untouched until an external consultant discovered hydration represented the largest unmonetised component by volume.\
By the time a sixty-three-year-old Cloudborne locksmith called Milo Garp starved to death with his thumb resting obediently on an active Grasp screen, Sustain Basic delivered enough nutrition to prevent Grasp Incorporated being successfully sued in eleven of Ulyssia\'92s thirteen commercial jurisdictions.\
The other two were regarded as extremist.\
Aurelia Pike approved the report at 7:14 on a Tuesday morning.\
She did not read it.\
Aurelia was founder, executive chair and principal shareholder of Grasp Incorporated, which made her sufficiently rich that money had ceased functioning as a medium of exchange and become a weather system around her.\
Her principal residence occupied the top nine floors of Pike Spire, although 
\f2\i occupied
\f1\i0  overstated the matter. Aurelia herself required remarkably little space. The rest existed to prevent anyone else being nearby.\
Her bedroom alone extended over three hundred square metres and contained one bed, two chairs and a seventeenth-century French writing desk at which nobody had written anything since the seventeenth century.\
The desk had cost more than a Cloudborne hospital.\
The hospital had subsequently been closed.\
Aurelia woke every morning with her right hand resting on her uGrasp Infinity 4.\
The Infinity woke first.\
During the night it maintained her blood glucose, electrolyte balance, metabolic signalling and sleep architecture. At precisely 6:59:55 it began feeding a microscopic stimulant dose through the skin of her palm. At seven it brightened the room, suppressed melatonin and released a neurochemical suggestion that existence was, on balance, worth continuing.\
The suggestion was stronger on weekdays.\
Aurelia opened her eyes.\
\'93Good morning, Aurelia,\'94 the Infinity said.\
\'93Don\'92t.\'94\
The Infinity stopped being cheerful.\
Aurelia lifted it from its charging cradle without breaking skin contact. Nobody who had used Grasp for more than five years casually broke skin contact.\
People still dropped devices.\
They just tended to drop with them.\
Grasp called the condition Tactile Continuity Dependency, after Legal rejected the engineering department\'92s preferred description, Catastrophic Contact Withdrawal.\
Three to seven seconds after prolonged users lost contact with an active screen, consciousness became negotiable.\
Ten seconds could produce coma.\
Grasp maintained that the phenomenon had nothing whatsoever to do with addiction. Addiction was an ugly, judgemental word from the age of cigarettes and gambling machines. Tactile Continuity Dependency was an adaptive metabolic relationship between human beings and technology.\
The company had successfully defended this distinction in court.\
Twice.\
Aurelia took her Infinity into the shower.\
It was waterproof to four kilometres.\
This was excessive for most bathrooms but invaluable to advertising.\
At 7:26, with one hand on the Infinity and another being dressed by a human being whose employment title was Personal Presentation Associate, Aurelia reviewed the launch schedule for the Grasp Continuum.\
Continuum was the first device Grasp described as 
\f2\i permanent
\f1\i0 .\
Nobody in Engineering knew what that meant.\
Marketing had been very clear about it.\
The launch would occur at noon in the Celestial Ballroom, two hundred and eighty floors above the Layer. Four thousand guests would attend physically. Another estimated 1.8 billion would attend through their devices, which was considerably safer because some physical guests had opinions.\
The Continuum had no ports, no seams, no replaceable components and no identifiable means of opening it.\
Its advertised service life was thirty years.\
Its warranty lasted eighteen months.\
Aurelia approved that too.\
By 8:10 she was aboard her hover-copt, crossing the city through brilliant sunlight while several million people below navigated streets they could no longer see because one hand contained their nutrition, identity, finances, entertainment, employment and most of their friends.\
Pedestrian deaths had initially increased.\
Then autonomous vehicles improved.\
Then pedestrians became autonomous too, in the sense that they moved according to whichever application had possession of their attention.\
Aurelia watched Grasp Tower approach.\
The building pierced the Layer like an accusation.\
Her Infinity vibrated.\
